STATE MONADS
============

WHY THEY EXIST
--------------
Many Section 4 problems require threading a state value (a stack, a memory
block, a register file, a letterbox) through a sequence of operations.
Without a monad you end up writing "let x, s1 = f s0" repeatedly.
The state monad hides that plumbing.


THE STANDARD DEFINITION - MEMORISE THIS
-----------------------------------------
Every exam uses the exact same shape:

    type StateMonad<'a> = SM of (State -> ('a * State) option)

    let ret x         = SM (fun s -> Some (x, s))
    let fail          = SM (fun _ -> None)

    let bind f (SM a) : StateMonad<'b> =
        SM (fun s ->
            match a s with
            | None         -> None
            | Some (x, s') ->
                let (SM g) = f x
                g s')

    let (>>=)  x f = bind f x
    let (>>>=) x y = x >>= (fun _ -> y)   // sequence, discard left result

The option wrapper models failure (e.g. popping an empty stack, reading an
out-of-bounds address). When there is no failure mode the exam sometimes uses
'a * State directly, but the option version is more common.


WRITING PRIMITIVES
------------------
Once bind and ret exist, every other operation is just an SM that reads or
modifies the state:

    // Read something from state without changing it
    let getReg r =
        SM (fun st -> Some (Map.tryFind r st.regs |> Option.defaultValue 0, st))

    // Modify state, return unit
    let setReg r v =
        SM (fun st -> Some ((), { st with regs = Map.add r v st.regs }))

The pattern is always:
    SM (fun s -> Some (result, newState))   for success
    SM (fun _ -> None)                      for unconditional failure


CHAINING WITH >>= AND >>>=
---------------------------
Use >>= when you need the result of the left side:

    evalExpr e1 >>= (fun v1 ->
    evalExpr e2 >>= (fun v2 ->
    ret (v1 + v2)))

Use >>>= when you don't need the result (sequencing effects):

    setReg "a" 5 >>>= advancePC >>>= evalProg

>>>= is shorthand for >>= (fun _ -> ...).
Use it whenever the left-hand side produces unit or you simply don't
need its value.


THE COMPUTATION EXPRESSION BUILDER - ALSO MEMORISE
----------------------------------------------------
The builder is identical in every exam:

    type StateBuilder() =
        member this.Bind(f, x)    = bind x f      // arguments are FLIPPED vs bind
        member this.Return(x)     = ret x
        member this.ReturnFrom(x) = x
        member this.Combine(a, b) = a >>= (fun _ -> b)

    let state = StateBuilder()

Note the argument order in Bind: F# passes (monadicValue, continuationFunction)
to Bind, which then calls bind continuationFunction monadicValue.
This is why the arguments are flipped relative to your bind definition.


COMPUTATION EXPRESSION SYNTAX
------------------------------
Once the builder exists you can write monadic code that looks almost imperative.

    let rec evalExpr2 e = state {
        match e with
        | Num x    -> return x
        | Lookup v -> return! lookup2 v          // return! unwraps an existing SM
        | Plus (e1, e2) ->
            let! v1 = evalExpr2 e1               // let! binds the unwrapped result
            let! v2 = evalExpr2 e2
            return (v1 + v2)
    }

    let rec evalProg2 prog = state {
        match prog with
        | [] -> return ()
        | s :: rest ->
            do! evalStmnt2 s                     // do! for effects that return unit
            return! evalProg2 rest
    }


LET! vs DO! vs RETURN!
-----------------------
let! x = m      m produces a value you need
do! m           m produces unit, you just want the effect
return x        wrap a plain value into the monad
return! m       pass through an already-monadic value (use for tail calls)


COMMON MISTAKES
---------------
- Writing "return! ret x" - this double-wraps. Just write "return x".
- Using let! when the monad produces unit and then never using the binding.
  Use do! instead or you get a type error.
- Forgetting that >>>= discards the left result. If you need the value, use >>=.
- Implementing Bind with arguments the wrong way round. The flip is
  counterintuitive but every exam uses the same pattern, so memorise it:
      member this.Bind(f, x) = bind x f
